# PART 9 — SECURE NOTES SYSTEM

Implement a separate notes application integrated into the virtual world.

This is not a code editor.

It must not be able to execute code.

## 9.1 Notes Features

Users should be able to:

- Create notes.
- Rename notes.
- Organize notes into folders or notebooks.
- Edit note content.
- Save automatically.
- Search notes.
- Use rich text or safe Markdown.
- Add headings, lists, equations, and simple formatting.
- Optionally attach supported safe resources.
- Share notes with selected participants.
- Control read and write permissions.
- Create flashcards from selected notes.

Notes should have independent IDs and persistent content.

Support private and shared notes.

## 9.2 Strict Non-Executable Model

**Notes are untrusted documents, never executable instructions.**

A note can contain source code as inert text, but opening or rendering it must never execute that code.

Do not implement:

- An integrated terminal in the notes application.
- Code execution buttons.
- Dynamic JavaScript execution.
- Scriptable macros.
- Executable note plugins.
- Automatic shell commands.
- HTML event handlers.
- Unrestricted HTML rendering.
- Hidden execution through Markdown extensions.

Use a safe rendering pipeline.

Prefer Markdown parsing with strict sanitization and a restricted component allowlist.

Disable unsafe raw HTML.

Apply strict URL and attachment handling.

Never rely solely on client-side sanitization.

Use a restrictive Content Security Policy and appropriate server-side validation.

Protect against stored XSS, malicious links, path traversal, unauthorized file access, and unsafe attachment processing.

## 9.3 Separation from Projects

Notes and projects must have separate security models.

A note must never inherit the privileges of the coding environment.

Opening a note must not trigger project compilation.

AI processing of a note must not invoke executable project tools.

Converting a note into flashcards is a data transformation, not a code execution workflow.

